    "presentation": {
      "chief_complaint": "Diarrhea and poor intake",
      "hpi": "A 64-year-old woman with a remote kidney transplant is admitted for several days of watery diarrhea and poor intake. She is not admitted with a diagnosis of cytomegalovirus colitis. Home immunosuppression is tacrolimus 1 mg orally every 12 hours, mycophenolate mofetil 1000 mg orally twice daily, and prednisone 5 mg orally once daily. She also takes amlodipine 5 mg daily and atorvastatin 40 mg daily. Valganciclovir is not a home medicine. Admission weight is 64 kg. Blood pressure is 100/62 mm Hg and heart rate is 110 beats per minute. Creatinine is 1.8 mg/dL, above an outpatient baseline of 1.0 mg/dL. Potassium is 3.2 mmol/L. Stool culture did not grow a bacterial pathogen, and Clostridioides difficile toxin is negative. On hospital day 2, colonoscopy shows colitis and the biopsy immunohistochemistry is positive for cytomegalovirus. Plasma cytomegalovirus DNA is 8500 IU/mL. Mycophenolate was not administered after that biopsy. Ganciclovir 5 mg/kg intravenously every 12 hours was given after the biopsy; at 66 kg that dose is 330 mg. Potassium chloride, 40 mEq orally, was given for the admission potassium. Diarrhea slows to one or two formed stools a day. Discharge weight is 66 kg, creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL, and potassium is 4.0 mmol/L. The transplant pharmacist records creatinine clearance above 60 mL/min from the discharge age, weight, and creatinine. Valganciclovir 900 mg orally twice daily, given as 450 mg tablets, was given after the intravenous ganciclovir once creatinine clearance was above 60 mL/min. Tacrolimus 1 mg every 12 hours was given through the stay; a trough is 6.5 ng/mL and the transplant target on the chart is 5 to 8 ng/mL. Prednisone 5 mg daily was given through the stay. Transplant clinic is in 7 days.",
      "review_of_systems": null,
      "presenting_symptoms": [
        "Diarrhea"
      ],
      "symptom_duration": "several days",
      "symptom_course": "diarrhea slowed after volume repletion and antiviral treatment"
    },
